time="2026-11-05T14:04:13.082-05:00" level=info msg="Final message: \"In-Reply-To: <DS4PR07MB99533408605D9FED0E6B01F69BC36E2@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com>\\r\\nMIME-Version: 1.0\\r\\nReferences: <DS4PR07MB995334F8F9556759A51C6D5D00C3672@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com> <DS4PR07MB99533408605D9FED0E6B01F69BC36E2@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com>\\r\\nSubject: Re: Quick Ask for Retreat!\\r\\nTo: \\\"Luckenbill, Kelsey\\\" <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu>\\r\\nContent-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=\\\"617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb\\\"\\r\\nContent-Disposition: inline\\r\\n\\r\\n--617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb\\r\\nContent-Disposition: inline\\r\\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=\\\"UTF-8\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nHi Kelsey,\\n\\nJust wanted to let you know, unlike what I said earlier, I'll still be happy to help with stuff for the retreat even though the strike is still going on. I think this can count as one of the categories of tasks to not strike from.\\n\\nSee you tomorrow,\\n\\nCaleb\\n\\nOn Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:55:00 -0400, \\\"Luckenbill, Kelsey\\\" <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu> said:\\n> Thanks all!\\n>\\n> Ok, so my idea is to have these questions printed for you guys for the faculty panel. Anyone can ask questions, but I do think we will find there are not that many questions coming from the audience, so if there is a lull in questions/conversation, that is where you guys would jump in. Here are some of the questions I think would work (you can add your own too- just trying to make your lives as easy as possible):\\n>\\n> Primary Topic: Research and scientific direction\\n>\\n>   *\\n> What problems in systems biology do you think are currently underappreciated but likely to become important in the next five to ten years?\\n>   *\\n> What types of data or technologies do you think are currently changing the way systems biology is practiced?\\n>   *\\n> Are there areas where you think the field is overemphasizing certain methods—for example, machine learning, single-cell data, or multi-omics?\\n>   *\\n> What are the biggest barriers to translating systems biology findings into clinical, industrial, or public-health applications?\\n>\\n> Second theme: The skills that scientists should develop to become future scientific leaders, and how to acquire them. Mentorship and graduate training\\n>\\n>   *\\n> What qualities do you value most in a successful PhD student, beyond technical ability?\\n>   *\\n> How can students take ownership of their research while still benefiting from close mentorship?\\n>   *\\n> What is the best way for students to approach faculty when they are stuck on a problem or need to change directions?\\n>   *\\n> What skills do you wish more systems biology students developed earlier in their training?\\n>   *\\n> What is your advice for building a strong scientific identity when working in a highly interdisciplinary field?\\n>\\n> Again- I will have this printed!\\n>\\n> Other items:\\n>\\n> I'm also going to send you guys a list of who all is supposed to be on the morning bus. I figure between you all, you will know most of the names/people!\\n>\\n> For the moderators, it is really just introducing the person's name and their lab- and maybe shutting down questions if they go over time, but I will be there to help with that!\\n> We will use this for G5 talks and for the G2 lightning talks.\\n> I will let you know if I need to put you in a moderator!\\n>\\n> Thanks again for helping with this!\\n>\\n> P.S. If you see people in the program please ask them to bring games for Friday night!\\n> P.P.S. Confirmed that you all will have single rooms 🙂\\n>\\n> ________________________________\\n> From: Luckenbill, Kelsey <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu>\\n> Sent: Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:03 PM\\n> To: Lindgren, Caleb <clindgren@g.harvard.edu>; Hirschmann, Maile <mailejim@g.harvard.edu>; Furman, Max <Maxwell.Furman@wyss.harvard.edu>; Wiesenfeld, Sophia <swiesenfeld@g.harvard.edu>; Nick Hilgert <nhilgert@g.harvard.edu>\\n> Subject: Quick Ask for Retreat!\\n>\\n> Hey guys!\\n>\\n> Hope you are doing well :)\\n> I have a special task for you guys, if you are up to it--\\n>\\n> I need a few students to do the following and thought you would be excellent at it:\\n>\\n>   *\\n> Check attendance on the bus morning of retreat (I have to go down Thursday to set up)\\n>   *\\n> Be moderators for G5 talks (just intro for them)\\n>   *\\n> Ask questions for faculty panel\\n>      *\\n> More about this- we have Galit Lahav, Sean Megason, Markus Basan, and Tim Mitchison attending, along with Allon and Michael. I thought it might be a good idea to do a quick-ish panel discussion with some pre-determined questions students can ask them. I have already brainstormed some questions which I will send over to you once I get your \\\"Ok\\\" to help ask the questions, but feel free to add your own!\\n>\\n> I picked you guys specifically as I want a slight mix of G-years and think you guys are more natural public speakers in the program.\\n>\\n> As a thank you for helping me, should you choose to, I have you all in single rooms. 🙂\\n>\\n> Let me know if you are on board and I can send over the list of questions I came up with and we can go from there for the panel!\\n>\\n> Thanks,\\n> Kelsey\\n>\\n> Kelsey Luckenbill\\n> Program Administrator for Systems, Synthetic, and Quantitative Biology PhD Program\\n> Harvard University\\n> kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu\\n>\\r\\n--617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb--\\r\\n\"" pid=18314
time="2026-11-05T14:04:13.579-05:00" level=info msg="History for 2013882 INBOX" pid=5243
time="2026-11-05T14:04:13.626-05:00" level=info msg="RPC> gmail.Users.Messages.Send(email=\"me\" threadID=\"1a15ae91566bc975\" msg=\"In-Reply-To: <DS4PR07MB99533408605D9FED0E6B01F69BC36E2@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com>\\r\\nMIME-Version: 1.0\\r\\nReferences: <DS4PR07MB995334F8F9556759A51C6D5D00C3672@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com> <DS4PR07MB99533408605D9FED0E6B01F69BC36E2@DS4PR07MB995334.namprd07.prod.outlook.com>\\r\\nSubject: Re: Quick Ask for Retreat!\\r\\nTo: \\\"Luckenbill, Kelsey\\\" <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu>\\r\\nContent-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=\\\"617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb\\\"\\r\\nContent-Disposition: inline\\r\\n\\r\\n--617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb\\r\\nContent-Disposition: inline\\r\\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=\\\"UTF-8\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nHi Kelsey,\\n\\nJust wanted to let you know, unlike what I said earlier, I'll still be happy to help with stuff for the retreat even though the strike is still going on. I think this can count as one of the categories of tasks to not strike from.\\n\\nSee you tomorrow,\\n\\nCaleb\\n\\nOn Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:55:00 -0400, \\\"Luckenbill, Kelsey\\\" <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu> said:\\n> Thanks all!\\n>\\n> Ok, so my idea is to have these questions printed for you guys for the faculty panel. Anyone can ask questions, but I do think we will find there are not that many questions coming from the audience, so if there is a lull in questions/conversation, that is where you guys would jump in. Here are some of the questions I think would work (you can add your own too- just trying to make your lives as easy as possible):\\n>\\n> Primary Topic: Research and scientific direction\\n>\\n>   *\\n> What problems in systems biology do you think are currently underappreciated but likely to become important in the next five to ten years?\\n>   *\\n> What types of data or technologies do you think are currently changing the way systems biology is practiced?\\n>   *\\n> Are there areas where you think the field is overemphasizing certain methods—for example, machine learning, single-cell data, or multi-omics?\\n>   *\\n> What are the biggest barriers to translating systems biology findings into clinical, industrial, or public-health applications?\\n>\\n> Second theme: The skills that scientists should develop to become future scientific leaders, and how to acquire them. Mentorship and graduate training\\n>\\n>   *\\n> What qualities do you value most in a successful PhD student, beyond technical ability?\\n>   *\\n> How can students take ownership of their research while still benefiting from close mentorship?\\n>   *\\n> What is the best way for students to approach faculty when they are stuck on a problem or need to change directions?\\n>   *\\n> What skills do you wish more systems biology students developed earlier in their training?\\n>   *\\n> What is your advice for building a strong scientific identity when working in a highly interdisciplinary field?\\n>\\n> Again- I will have this printed!\\n>\\n> Other items:\\n>\\n> I'm also going to send you guys a list of who all is supposed to be on the morning bus. I figure between you all, you will know most of the names/people!\\n>\\n> For the moderators, it is really just introducing the person's name and their lab- and maybe shutting down questions if they go over time, but I will be there to help with that!\\n> We will use this for G5 talks and for the G2 lightning talks.\\n> I will let you know if I need to put you in a moderator!\\n>\\n> Thanks again for helping with this!\\n>\\n> P.S. If you see people in the program please ask them to bring games for Friday night!\\n> P.P.S. Confirmed that you all will have single rooms 🙂\\n>\\n> ________________________________\\n> From: Luckenbill, Kelsey <kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu>\\n> Sent: Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:03 PM\\n> To: Lindgren, Caleb <clindgren@g.harvard.edu>; Hirschmann, Maile <mailejim@g.harvard.edu>; Furman, Max <Maxwell.Furman@wyss.harvard.edu>; Wiesenfeld, Sophia <swiesenfeld@g.harvard.edu>; Nick Hilgert <nhilgert@g.harvard.edu>\\n> Subject: Quick Ask for Retreat!\\n>\\n> Hey guys!\\n>\\n> Hope you are doing well :)\\n> I have a special task for you guys, if you are up to it--\\n>\\n> I need a few students to do the following and thought you would be excellent at it:\\n>\\n>   *\\n> Check attendance on the bus morning of retreat (I have to go down Thursday to set up)\\n>   *\\n> Be moderators for G5 talks (just intro for them)\\n>   *\\n> Ask questions for faculty panel\\n>      *\\n> More about this- we have Galit Lahav, Sean Megason, Markus Basan, and Tim Mitchison attending, along with Allon and Michael. I thought it might be a good idea to do a quick-ish panel discussion with some pre-determined questions students can ask them. I have already brainstormed some questions which I will send over to you once I get your \\\"Ok\\\" to help ask the questions, but feel free to add your own!\\n>\\n> I picked you guys specifically as I want a slight mix of G-years and think you guys are more natural public speakers in the program.\\n>\\n> As a thank you for helping me, should you choose to, I have you all in single rooms. 🙂\\n>\\n> Let me know if you are on board and I can send over the list of questions I came up with and we can go from there for the panel!\\n>\\n> Thanks,\\n> Kelsey\\n>\\n> Kelsey Luckenbill\\n> Program Administrator for Systems, Synthetic, and Quantitative Biology PhD Program\\n> Harvard University\\n> kelsey_luckenbill@fas.harvard.edu\\n>\\r\\n--617b1e8ad4258e60f8be28b88375d4cd28087d4ecbd00ef3aca1b30ccfcb--\\r\\n\") => <nil> 544.322427ms" pid=18314
